<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.2.1: Selecting the Best Line</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00713</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.2.1: Selecting the Best Line</md:title>
    <md:uuid>35bda0a1-b6a7-4b0a-bf13-dee82484090f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Which of the lines is the better fit for the data in each scatter plot? Be prepared
            to show your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing wins on the x-axis and number of runs allowed per game on the y-axis, with data points, a solid blue line trending downward, and a dashed black line trending upward.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_04_02_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">The solid line is the better fit for the data since it goes through the middle of
            the data and follows the negative trend of the data with very few points a far distance
            from the line of best fit.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing a positive correlation between the average score from 10 surveys on the x-axis and the average amount spent on dinner on the y-axis, with a solid blue trend line and a dashed black line for reference.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_04_02_02.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">The solid line is the better fit for the data since it goes through the middle of
            the data with approximately equal numbers of data points on either side of the line.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">
            <media alt="Scatter plot showing the cost of gas at a local station versus the price of crude oil per barrel, with data points, a black dashed trendline, and a blue fitted line, both indicating a positive correlation.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_04_02_03.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">

          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00010">The dashed line is the better fit for the data since it goes through the middle of
            the data with a slope that follows the trend of the data. The solid line has a lot of
            points above the line to start and a lot of points below the line at the end. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">
            <media alt="A scatter plot that shows the number of floors in a building on the x-axis and the height of the building in feet on the y-axis. Two lines are also drawn to approximate the line of best fit: a solid blue line and a black dashed line.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_04_02_04.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">The dashed line is the better fit for the data since it goes through the middle of
            the data, follows the trend of the data, and has a similar number of points on each side
            that are not far from the line. The solid line fits the first and last point perfectly,
            but most of the data are above the line.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
